[38;5;231m⏺[39m Third fork: [1mwhat the stored value actually is.[0m Right now [38;5;153mlogin[39m returns [38;5;153m{ success: true, user: username }[39m — there is no
  ID anywhere in the repo, so the spec has to say where one comes from.

  - [1mUse the username as the ID[0m — zero new concepts, works immediately. But it fuses identity with a display name: if
    usernames ever become editable, every previously-stored ID silently starts pointing at the wrong person, and you
    can't detect it after the fact. Cheap now, expensive to unwind.
  - [1mServer-returned ID, stubbed for now[0m — [38;5;153mlogin[39m returns an explicit [38;5;153muserId[39m field alongside [38;5;153msuccess[39m; the stub produces an
    obviously-fake value until a real [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m exists. The interface is correct from day one, so wiring the real
    backend later touches only the stub. Usernames stay display-only.
  - [1mClient-generated opaque ID[0m — mint a UUID at login and store it. Opaque and unlinked to the username, but it
    identifies [3ma login on this tab[0m, not a person: the same human gets a different ID on every login and every device,
    which makes it useless for "who logged in" the moment you have more than one session.

  I recommend the stubbed server-returned ID. It costs about the same as the username option today, keeps [38;5;153muserId[39m a real
  identity rather than a name, and the one place that has to change when the backend lands is the stub that's already
  fake.
